### What is a string rotation?

A string rotation takes some prefix of a string and moves it to the end. For example, rotating `SALARY` by two positions gives `LARYAS` because the prefix `SA` moves to the back. Every string has exactly n possible rotations including itself (rotation by zero positions). Two strings are rotations of each other if and only if one can be obtained from the other by this operation.

```java
package com.bank.core;

// "SALARY" rotated by 0: "SALARY"
// "SALARY" rotated by 1: "ALARYS"
// "SALARY" rotated by 2: "LARYAS"
// "SALARY" rotated by 3: "ARYSAL"
// "SALARY" rotated by 4: "RYSALA"
// "SALARY" rotated by 5: "YSALAR"
```

### Why does concatenating a string with itself work?

When you write s1 twice in a row, every possible rotation of s1 appears as a contiguous substring of that doubled string. For `SALARY`, the doubled form is `SALARYSALARY`, and inside it you can find `ALARYS` starting at index 1, `LARYAS` starting at index 2, and so on. Each rotation starts at offset k in the doubled string where k is the rotation amount.

This works because the doubled string contains all n characters of the second copy following all n characters of the first. A rotation by k takes the last (n-k) characters of the first copy and the first k characters of the second copy, which form a contiguous block of length n inside the doubled string.

```java
package com.bank.core;

// s1 = "SALARY"
// s1 + s1 = "SALARYSALARY"
//            ^^^^^^         -> rotation by 0: SALARY
//             ^^^^^^        -> rotation by 1: ALARYS
//              ^^^^^^       -> rotation by 2: LARYAS
//               ^^^^^^      -> rotation by 3: ARYSAL
//                ^^^^^^     -> rotation by 4: RYSALA
//                 ^^^^^^    -> rotation by 5: YSALAR
```

### How do you implement it in Java?

The implementation is a two-step check: first verify that both strings have the same length, then test whether s2 is a substring of s1+s1. In Java the simplest form uses the `contains` method on the concatenated string.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class RotationChecker {

    public boolean areRotations(String s1, String s2) {
        if (s1 == null || s2 == null) return false;
        if (s1.length() != s2.length()) return false;
        String doubled = s1 + s1;
        return doubled.contains(s2);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        RotationChecker checker = new RotationChecker();
        String code1 = "SALARY";
        String code2 = "LARYAS";
        System.out.println(code1 + " and " + code2
            + " are rotations: " + checker.areRotations(code1, code2));

        String code3 = "SALARY";
        String code4 = "LARYES";
        System.out.println(code3 + " and " + code4
            + " are rotations: " + checker.areRotations(code3, code4));
    }
}
```

```text
SALARY and LARYAS are rotations: true
SALARY and LARYES are rotations: false
```

### Why is the length check mandatory?

Without the length check, a shorter string could appear inside the doubled longer string and be falsely reported as a rotation. For example, `AB` would be found inside `ABCABC` (the doubling of `ABC`), but `AB` is not a rotation of `ABC`. The length check ensures that any substring match spans the full rotation length.

```java
package com.bank.core;

// WRONG: no length check
public class WrongRotationChecker {
    public boolean areRotations(String s1, String s2) {
        return (s1 + s1).contains(s2); // "AB" found in "ABCABC" -> false positive
    }
}

// CORRECT: length check first
public class CorrectRotationChecker {
    public boolean areRotations(String s1, String s2) {
        if (s1.length() != s2.length()) return false;
        return (s1 + s1).contains(s2);
    }
}
```

### How does this compare to a brute-force rotation check?

The brute-force approach generates all n rotations of s1 and compares each one to s2. Generating each rotation is O(n) and there are n rotations, so brute force is O(n^2). The doubled-string approach reduces the problem to a single substring search, which is O(n) with KMP or O(n^2) worst case with naive search but typically fast in practice.

| Approach | Time complexity | Space complexity | Code complexity |
|---|---|---|---|
| Brute force (all rotations) | O(n^2) | O(n) per rotation | Simple but slow |
| Doubled string + contains | O(n^2) worst case | O(n) for doubled string | One-liner |
| Doubled string + KMP | O(n) guaranteed | O(n) for doubled + pattern table | Linear guarantee |

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class BruteForceRotationChecker {

    public boolean areRotations(String s1, String s2) {
        if (s1.length() != s2.length()) return false;
        int n = s1.length();
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            // Rotation by i: s1[i..n-1] + s1[0..i-1]
            String rotated = s1.substring(i) + s1.substring(0, i);
            if (rotated.equals(s2)) return true;
        }
        return false;
    }
}
```

### What are the edge cases?

Several edge cases require attention: two empty strings are trivially rotations of each other, a string is always a rotation of itself (rotation by zero), null inputs should be handled gracefully, and case sensitivity should be defined upfront.

```java
package com.bank.core;

// Edge case 1: both empty -> true (trivially rotations)
// Edge case 2: "SALARY" and "SALARY" -> true (rotation by 0)
// Edge case 3: one null -> false (null guard)
// Edge case 4: "Salary" and "arySal" -> depends on case sensitivity
// Edge case 5: "A" and "A" -> true (single character)
// Edge case 6: different lengths -> false immediately
```

### What about using KMP for guaranteed linear time?

Java's `String.contains` uses a naive substring search that can be O(n*m) in the worst case. For a guaranteed O(n) solution, you search for s2 inside s1+s1 using the Knuth-Morris-Pratt algorithm, which preprocesses the pattern s2 into a failure table and scans the text in one pass without backtracking.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class KMPRotationChecker {

    public boolean areRotations(String s1, String s2) {
        if (s1.length() != s2.length()) return false;
        if (s1.isEmpty()) return true;
        String text = s1 + s1;
        return kmpSearch(text, s2);
    }

    private boolean kmpSearch(String text, String pattern) {
        int n = text.length();
        int m = pattern.length();
        int[] lps = buildLPS(pattern);
        int j = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            while (j > 0 && text.charAt(i) != pattern.charAt(j)) {
                j = lps[j - 1];
            }
            if (text.charAt(i) == pattern.charAt(j)) {
                j++;
            }
            if (j == m) return true;
        }
        return false;
    }

    private int[] buildLPS(String pattern) {
        int m = pattern.length();
        int[] lps = new int[m];
        int len = 0;
        int i = 1;
        while (i < m) {
            if (pattern.charAt(i) == pattern.charAt(len)) {
                lps[i++] = ++len;
            } else if (len > 0) {
                len = lps[len - 1];
            } else {
                lps[i++] = 0;
            }
        }
        return lps;
    }
}
```

### What about a banking use case?

At the National Bank of India, different settlement systems record the same circular account identifier starting at different positions. Rajesh needs to determine whether two identifiers represent the same underlying circular code during reconciliation.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class CircularCodeReconciler {

    private final RotationChecker checker = new RotationChecker();

    public boolean isSameCircularCode(String code1, String code2) {
        if (code1 == null || code2 == null) return false;
        return checker.areRotations(code1, code2);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        CircularCodeReconciler reconciler = new CircularCodeReconciler();

        System.out.println(reconciler.isSameCircularCode("HDFC2024", "2024HDFC"));
        System.out.println(reconciler.isSameCircularCode("ICICI001", "001ICICI"));
        System.out.println(reconciler.isSameCircularCode("HDFC2024", "HDFC2025"));
    }
}
```

```text
true
true
false
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer should know that the doubled-string approach is elegant but hides a cost: the `contains` call in Java uses a naive search, so for performance-critical code on very long strings the KMP-based variant is the right choice. The approach also generalizes: checking if one array is a rotation of another uses the same doubled-array technique, and the same idea applies to circular buffers in systems programming.

In production reconciliation, case sensitivity and encoding must be defined upfront, and the comparison should normalize both strings before checking rotation to avoid locale-specific surprises.

```java
package com.bank.core;

// Production checklist:
// 1. Normalize both strings (trim, case, encoding)
// 2. Length check first (fast rejection)
// 3. Use KMP for guaranteed O(n) on long identifiers
// 4. Handle null and empty inputs gracefully
// 5. Log mismatches for reconciliation auditing
```
